\section*{For rehearsal}
\begin{rehearsal}
\pnote{The words} Luther’s Small Catechism taught children to commend
themselves at bedtime into God’s hands and to ask for his angel against the
enemy. Polish Lutherans translated that prayer, and Trzecieski’s stanzas
3 and 4 follow it almost clause by clause; his first stanza recalls Compline,
the old night office. Sing the old forms as printed (\pl{zmierzka, naszem
strażem, nawięcej, wszytki, swoję, Jesu}) with modern pronunciation.
\pl{Ześli} in stanza 3 means ‘send’.

\pnote{Stanzas} All four go to the same music. Stanza 1 is under the notes.
For stanzas 2–4, give each syllable the notes its counterpart has in
stanza 1.

\pnote{Pitch and voices} Written ranges: Cantus c′–d″, Altus d–a′, Tenor
d–e′, Bassus G–a. At written pitch the piece suits men with a high Cantus, or
a choir of low voices. A mixed choir will usually sing it a minor third
higher. Choose the pitch for the singers; the notation does not change.

\pnote{Tempo} A minim of about 66–80, felt two to the semibreve and four to
the bar. Keep the semiminim runs light.

\pnote{The score} Note values and pitch are those of the 1550s print. A bar is
one breve. Bar lines run between the staves only, so a note that crosses one
is held for its full value. The small staff before each voice shows the
original clef and first note. Sharps above the staff are editorial.

\end{rehearsal}
\footcolophon
